\section{Detached editing}{

With detached editing enabled, edits made while checked out at an
earlier version are recorded as changes that follow that version,
concurrent with everything that came after it. Returning to the
latest version with \code{doc_checkout(doc)} merges the branch with the
rest of the history, as if another replica had made those edits.

To keep the branches distinct, the document takes a new random peer
id at each checkout while this is enabled, and keeps the last one
after returning to the latest version, so do not rely on
\code{\link[=doc_peer_id]{doc_peer_id()}} staying fixed. The setting belongs to this
\code{loro_doc} object and is not saved in exports.
}

\section{Changes}{

\code{doc_changes()} returns a tibble, in causal order, with columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{peer}, \code{counter}: the id of the change's first operation.
\item \code{lamport}: its logical timestamp.
\item \code{length}: the number of operations in the change.
\item \code{timestamp}: when it was committed (\code{NA} if not recorded).
\item \code{message}: the commit message (\code{NA} if none).
\item \code{frontiers}: a list column holding the version just after the
change, suitable for \code{doc_checkout()}, \code{doc_revert_to()},
\code{doc_diff()}, and \code{doc_fork()}.
}

Consecutive commits by one peer that carry the same message, or no
message, may be stored as a single change, so rows do not correspond
one-to-one with calls to \code{\link[=doc_commit]{doc_commit()}}. An empty message is the
same as no message, and a commit with nothing to commit is dropped
along with its message.
}

\section{Diffs}{

\code{doc_diff()} returns a named list with one element per changed
container, named by container id (see \code{\link[=container_id]{container_id()}}). Each
element has a \code{type} and:
\itemize{
\item for text, \code{delta}: delta ops as in \code{\link[=text_apply_delta]{text_apply_delta()}};
\item for a list or movable list (both have type \code{"list"}), \code{delta}: ops with \code{retain} or \code{delete} counts, or
\code{insert} holding a list of new elements;
\item for a map, \code{updated}: a named list of the new values of keys that
were set, and \code{deleted}: a character vector of keys that were
removed;
\item for a counter, \code{increment}: the net amount added;
\item for a tree, \code{diff}: a list of node changes, each with \code{node}, an
\code{action} (\code{"create"}, \code{"move"}, or \code{"delete"}), and the \code{parent}
and 1-based \code{index} it now has and, for moves and deletes, the
\code{old_parent} and \code{old_index} it had (\code{NA} parents are the top
level).
}

Child containers appear by their contents.
}

